---
title: "Reading in Data"
format: pdf
---

Today we are going to jump back into ggplot to visualize some data! My goal is to make it as pink as possible. 

Heres where things stand right now: I have read in all three time periods of the protest data, found the "topic" columns and renamed them so they all match (why they called the same columns different titles for different years is beyond me!). I decided to drop events that had no topic as we determined that untagged events were evenly spread across years. I cleaned the data to only have events tagged "environment" or "climate," learning that many climate-type events have broader tags attached to them as well. Finally, we counted climate/environment protests per month. 

Today, I want to find the "wave" months and build the "wave" and "ordinary" pairs. As a reminder, a wave pair is a "before" and "after" month pair where the after month is a protest wave, defined as a month in the top 10% of monthly protest counts. Note that the 10% is arbitrarily picked for the time being, and further projects along this line of thinking should conduct sensitivity tests around this.

(Note from Brie of the future: We get sidetracked due to some outliers.)

Some of this document is going to repeat last week's data, as I am writing this on the same file. I'll indicate where my new stuff starts.

The first step is to read in the Crowd Counting Consortium data. I downloaded the three available phases; 2017-2020, 2021-2024, and 2025's currently availiable data.
```{r}
#| echo: false 
library(tidyverse)
library(lubridate)
```
```{r}
#| echo: false 
ccc_files <- list.files("data/raw/ccc", pattern = "\\.csv(\\.gz)?$", full.names = TRUE)
ccc_files
```
I want to stack the three csv files into one table. 
```{r}
ccc_raw <- ccc_files |>
  map(\(f) read_csv(f, col_types = cols(.default = col_character())) |>
        rename_with(tolower)) |>
  bind_rows()

nrow(ccc_raw)   # number of protest events
ncol(ccc_raw)   # number of columns

ccc_raw |>
  select(date, locality, state, issues, issue_tags) |>
  head()
```
I have run into a problem where the three phases all call the "protest inciting issue" different column names. The older phases use "issues," whereas the newer phases use "issues_tags." 
```{r}
#| echo: false 
ccc_raw |>
  summarise(
    issues       = sum(!is.na(issues)),
    issue_tags   = sum(!is.na(issue_tags)),
    tags_summary = sum(!is.na(issue_tags_summary)),
    total_rows   = n()
  )

ccc_raw <- 
  ccc_raw |>
  mutate(topic = coalesce(issue_tags, issues, issue_tags_summary))

sum(is.na(ccc_raw$topic))

ccc_raw |> count(topic, sort = TRUE) |> head(20)
sum(is.na(ccc_raw$topic))

climate <- ccc_raw |>
  filter(str_detect(str_to_lower(topic), "environment|climate"))

nrow(climate)
climate |> count(topic, sort = TRUE) |> head(15)
```
I want to interrogate the distribution of untagged events to decide what I will do with the missing data.
```{r}
#| echo: false 
ccc_raw |>
  filter(is.na(topic)) |>
  mutate(year = str_sub(date, 1, 4)) |>
  count(year)

mean(is.na(ccc_raw$topic))
```
I have decided that I will exclude events with no issue tags (about 2.4% of events, spread across all years)

Lets now count how many climate and environmental protests happened in each month. This will allow me to find the protest waves (a month in the top 10% of monthly counts), which will tell me what wikipedia data I need to download.

```{r}
#| echo: false 
climate <- 
  climate |>
  mutate(
    date  = as_date(parse_date_time(date, orders = c("ymd", "mdy"))),
    month = floor_date(date, "month")
  )

nrow(climate)
range(climate$date, na.rm = TRUE)
sum(is.na(climate$date))


climate <- climate |>
  mutate(
    date  = as_date(parse_date_time(date, orders = c("ymd", "mdy"))),
    month = floor_date(date, "month")
  )

nrow(climate)
range(climate$date, na.rm = TRUE)
sum(is.na(climate$date))
```

```{r}
#| echo: false 
monthly <-
  climate |>
  count(month, name = "protests")
monthly |> arrange(month)
head(10)
```
# NEW STUFF!

Lets visualize some protests!

(Note from Brie of the future: I start with bar graphs and quickly realize they look bad, then decide to do a heat map.)


```{r}
#| echo: false
climate |>
  count(month, name = "protests") |>
  ggplot(aes(x = month, y = protests)) +
  geom_col(fill = "#F06BA5", width = 25) +
  scale_x_date(date_breaks = "1 year", date_labels = "%Y") +
  labs(
    x = NULL,
    y = "Climate protests per month",
    title = "Climate and environmental protests per month"
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank())

climate |>
  count(month, name = "protests") |>
  ggplot(aes(x = month, y = protests)) +
  geom_line(color = "#F4B6C8", linewidth = 1) +
  geom_point(color = "#E0218A", size = 1.8) +
  scale_x_date(date_breaks = "1 year", date_labels = "%Y") +
  labs(
    x = NULL,
    y = "Climate protests per month",
    title = "Climate and environmental protests per month"
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
```
Although those graph technically shows use the number of protest per month, I personally find it hard to actually read. 
```{r}
#| echo: false
climate |>
  count(month, name = "protests") |>
  mutate(
    year = factor(year(month)),
    mon  = month(month, label = TRUE)
  ) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = mon, y = fct_rev(year), fill = protests)) +
  geom_tile(color = "white") +
  geom_text(aes(label = protests), size = 3) +
  scale_fill_gradient(low = "#FFF0F6", high = "#E0218A", name = "Protests") +
  labs(x = NULL, y = NULL, title = "Climate protests per month") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(panel.grid = element_blank())
```

I like this heat map, but the colours are similar for the lower protest count months... Lets try to find a solution. Something about capping the gradient so the outliers are not so significantly the only things at the high point of the heat map.

Trying to visualize this data makes me realize I need to figure out the distribution of protest amounts. According to the internet, the best solution for me is to set a cap on the colour range near the 90th to 95th percentile. 
```{r}
climate |>
  count(month, name = "protests") |>
  pull(protests) |>
  quantile(c(.1, .25, .5, .75, .9, .95, 1))
```
Half of our months have 122 protest or fewer, 90% are under 331, but the upper percentile has 3636!! Lets try setting the cap at the 90th percentile.  This means the top 10% will get the darkest pink, and everything else should be more visible on the heat map. For the record, I am also very curious what happened to make some months such an outlier, but I am going to deal with that later. 
```{r}
cap <- 350

climate |>
  count(month, name = "protests") |>
  mutate(
    year = factor(year(month)),
    mon  = month(month, label = TRUE)
  ) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = mon, y = fct_rev(year), fill = protests)) +
  geom_tile(color = "white") +
  geom_text(aes(label = protests), size = 3) +
  scale_fill_gradientn(
    colors = c("#FFF5F9", "#FAC4DA", "#F27BAE", "#E0218A", "#C2185B"),
    limits = c(0, cap),
    oob = scales::squish,
    breaks = c(0, 100, 200, 300, 350),
    labels = c("0", "100", "200", "300", "350+"),
    name = "Protests"
  ) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = NULL, title = "Climate protests per month") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(panel.grid = element_blank())

```
This looks nice! And revealed a new concern for me.... What caused these outliers? My American friends beside me have reminded me that, for exmaple, October 2025 is when the American government shut down and many government employees got furloughed, notably a lot of climate-related workers. I need to decide if this event is too much of an outlier for my purposes. I hate nuance!!!

--

It is now a few days later and I have decided to keep the large protest days - in short, I believe they represent the complex nature of "climate" related protests. Climate change is not separate from the instituions that enabled unevenly distributed environmental harms in the first place, so the multifacted protests may even aid my analysis by including that complexity outright. 



## Next step: find the protest waves

The Clickstream data starts in late 2017, so my study window begins in December 2017. The last month of CCC data (May 2026) is only partly covered, so the window ends at the last full month. Every month in the window has at least one protest, so no months needed to be filled in as zero. A protest wave is a month in the top 10% of monthly counts.


```{r}
#clickstream data needs two months to form a pair so we start in december 2017  
study_start <- ymd("2017-12-01")
# the ccc dataset is incomplete fro may 2026 so we are going to go back one month from the last recorded month (may 2026) to ensure we only use months 
study_end   <- floor_date(max(climate$date, na.rm = TRUE), "month") %m-% months(1)
study_end

monthly_window <- monthly |>
  filter(month >= study_start, month <= study_end)

nrow(monthly_window)
wave_cutoff <- quantile(monthly_window$protests, 0.90)
wave_cutoff

monthly_window <- monthly_window |>
  mutate(wave = protests >= wave_cutoff)

monthly_window |> count(wave)
monthly_window |> filter(wave) |> select(month, protests)
```
```{r}
# full list of wave months (print(n = Inf) shows every row)
monthly_window |> count(wave)
monthly_window |> filter(wave) |> select(month, protests) |> print(n = Inf)

# build the before/after pairs
month_pairs <- monthly_window |>
  mutate(
    before_month    = lag(month),
    before_protests = lag(protests),
    before_is_wave  = lag(wave)
  ) |>
  filter(!is.na(before_month)) |>   # drops Dec 2017, which only serves as a "before"
  transmute(
    before_month,
    after_month    = month,
    before_protests,
    after_protests = protests,
    before_is_wave,
    pair_type = if_else(wave, "wave", "ordinary")
  )

count(month_pairs, pair_type)
month_pairs |> filter(pair_type == "wave") |> count(before_is_wave)
```
We have now identified the "wave" months. Some notes:
- 11 total wave months, with. cutoff of 335 (March 2024)
- There is only one back to back wave (March and April 2022), meaning we have 10 "ordinary" pairs
- 4 of the 11 waves are in March, which signals to me that I investigate this pattern (seasonal/spring mobilizations?)
- 3 of the 11 months are in January, same question as above
-I also want to check if the CCC's tagging changed over time, as the most extreme waves are also quite recent. This could be because of the political events we spoke about earlier, but I want to make sure it is not an obvious data effect.

## Wave pairs
```{r}
#| echo: false
month_pairs <- monthly_window |>
  mutate(
    before_month    = lag(month),
    before_protests = lag(protests),
    before_is_wave  = lag(wave)
  ) |>
  filter(!is.na(before_month)) |>
  transmute(
    before_month,
    after_month    = month,
    before_protests,
    after_protests = protests,
    before_is_wave,
    pair_type = if_else(wave, "wave", "ordinary")
  )

count(month_pairs, pair_type)

#| echo: false
month_pairs |>
  filter(pair_type == "wave") |>
  transmute(
    `Wave month`       = format(after_month, "%b %Y"),
    `Month before`     = format(before_month, "%b %Y"),
    `Protests before`  = before_protests,
    `Protests in wave` = after_protests,
    `Change`           = after_protests - before_protests
  ) |>
  knitr::kable(
    format.args = list(big.mark = ","),
    align = "llrrr",
    caption = "Each protest wave month compared with the month before it"
  )






month_pairs |>
  filter(pair_type == "wave") |>
  mutate(label = format(after_month, "%b %Y")) |>
  select(label, after_month,
         `Month before` = before_protests,
         `Wave month`   = after_protests) |>
  pivot_longer(c(`Month before`, `Wave month`),
               names_to = "type", values_to = "protests") |>
  ggplot(aes(x = protests,
             y = fct_reorder(label, after_month, .desc = TRUE),
             fill = type)) +
  geom_col(position = position_dodge(width = 0.9)) +
  geom_text(aes(label = protests),
            position = position_dodge(width = 0.9),
            hjust = -0.2, size = 3) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("Wave month" = "#E0218A",
                               "Month before" = "#F8BBD6"),
                    name = NULL) +
  scale_x_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.1))) +
  labs(x = "Climate protests", y = NULL,
       title = "Protest wave months and the month before each") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom")
```





